Autonomous vehicles have gradually moved from test tracks onto public roads. Robotaxis have been carrying passengers in several cities, and driver-assistance systems have been deployed in commercial cars. More and more manufacturers are building these systems, and no two are alike. They run different sensors, different planners, and different driving policies, trained separately and opaque to one another. For a long time to come they will also share the road with human drivers rather than replace them, so any one car has to cope with mixed, heterogeneous traffic it neither controls nor can see inside. This makes how such vehicles communicate with each other important. But whether communication can emerge among these vehicles is still unsettled. This dissertation asks that question, in a small, controlled version of the problem.

Driving on a highway means constantly reacting to cars you cannot fully read. Whether a neighbour is cautious or assertive, how much risk they accept, how close they like to follow, none of this is visible directly. The usual approach in the field is perception, prediction, and planning: build a model of how nearby cars are likely to move, and plan around it @chen2022milestones. These prediction methods have improved a great deal, from simple rule-based simulation, to tracking a few possible intentions @bai2015intention @zhou2018joint, to learning a hidden driver type from data with neural-network encoders @liu2022learning @liu2024automatic @ma2021reinforcement.

But this whole line of work looks at only one side of the problem. The car asks "what will my neighbour do?" and stops there. A human driver also asks the other half: "what do our neighbours think I am, and is the way I drive shaping that belief?" How a driver acts matters as much as how it reads and responds to others, since that action is what lets other drivers form a belief about its type. Those interactions act as signals that help agents coordinate with each other @fox2018when @risto2017human @wang2020driver.

Take a simple two-car example. Car A is cautious and car B is assertive, and neither can see the other's type directly. The two approach a merge where only one can go first. B wants A to know that it will not back off, so it makes that intent legible in the only way available: it closes the gap quickly, holds a tight headway, and accelerates decisively towards the merge point. None of this is a message in any literal sense. It is simply how B drives, but executed firmly enough that the style itself announces an assertive type.

A reads that style, infers an assertive neighbour, and answers with its own: it eases off, opens the gap, and lets B take the space. That cautious response is itself legible to B, which now sees a driver willing to give way. The two settle without a collision and without any negotiation, as B goes first and A follows. Neither changed who it is. B stayed assertive and A stayed cautious; they did not meet in the middle on a shared style, but read each other's style and each kept its own, and that mutual reading let them coordinate. Neither car sent a message. The coordination came entirely from the visible manner of ordinary moves, where the same manoeuvre carries different information depending on _how_ it is executed.

